::JOUR_U01_L03_GP_Q01::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which statement best explains a journalism media transition? {
~A writer changes a headline without changing the platform.#Incorrect. That is an editing choice, not a media transition.
=Journalism moves from one communication tool or platform to another, changing technique or audience experience.#Correct. A media transition connects a tool change to how journalism is made or received.
~A story becomes longer but stays in the same format.#Incorrect. Length alone does not show a media technology transition.
~A journalist uses fewer facts than before.#Incorrect. A media transition should not mean weaker accuracy.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_GP_Q02::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
What changed when a monthly print publication added same-day website updates? {
~Readers could only get information more slowly.#Incorrect. Same-day web updates usually make information faster to access.
~Writers no longer needed to verify information.#Incorrect. Verification remains necessary across platforms.
=Journalists could publish shorter updates more quickly for audiences.#Correct. Web publishing changes speed, format, and audience access.
~The publication stopped needing headlines.#Incorrect. Headlines still guide readers online.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_GP_Q03::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Why do mobile alerts need careful wording? {
~They are too long for readers to understand.#Incorrect. Mobile alerts are usually short, not long.
~They replace all other journalism formats.#Incorrect. Mobile alerts are one format, not a replacement for all journalism.
~They do not need accuracy because they are brief.#Incorrect. Brief formats still need accuracy.
=They have limited space, so the message must be clear and accurate immediately.#Correct. Short digital formats require precise wording.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_GP_Q04::MLA.JOUR.MED.09
Which quality concern remains across print, radio, television, websites, and mobile alerts? {
=Accuracy#Correct. Accuracy remains central even when tools and formats change.
~Using the exact same layout#Incorrect. Layout can change by platform.
~Publishing only once a month#Incorrect. Publication timing changes across platforms.
~Avoiding all visuals#Incorrect. Some formats rely on visuals.
}

::JOUR_U01_L03_GP_Q05::MLA.JOUR.DIG.01
What should a digital presentation about media history include? {
~Only decoration, with no clear idea#Incorrect. A digital presentation needs coherent ideas.
~A list of tools without explaining any changes#Incorrect. The lesson requires explaining transitions, not just naming tools.
=A clear perspective about how a technology changed journalism technique or audience experience#Correct. The presentation should organize a clear explanation of change.
~Unsupported claims about one platform being perfect#Incorrect. Claims should be accurate and explained.
}
